                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d9c65d9b-15ed-4108-87aa-f21377a28985",
                                                "sense_id": "06a6a647-6d22-3ceb-b3f2-9a8bfea970a0",
                                                "text": "When the preparations were finished, he invited me with—“Now, sir, bring forward your chair.” And we all, including the rustic youth, drew round the table: an austere silence prevailing while we discussed our meal."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "39f0f46d-af78-49b6-93e1-65b7b402a5ed",
                                                "sense_id": "06a6a647-6d22-3ceb-b3f2-9a8bfea970a0",
                                                "text": "We sat quietly down and discussed a cold fowl that we had brought with us."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "dc621c8d-838a-45d6-9649-3b7f34566b44",
                                                "sense_id": "06a6a647-6d22-3ceb-b3f2-9a8bfea970a0",
                                                "text": "In the first room we entered, a soldier and a man, like a clerk or dominie, were discussing a bottle of red wine; they immediately sprang up and politely proffered us each a bumper."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "479af4b7-51ad-34e3-8a80-b58eca819710",
                                        "entry_id": "3d68c1d2-1ea7-356e-9ee0-b857147cf6c9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To examine or search thoroughly; to exhaust a remedy against, as against a principal debtor before proceeding against the surety."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "36e5d23a-e36f-3767-9e2d-05595b3173bf",
                                        "entry_id": "3d68c1d2-1ea7-356e-9ee0-b857147cf6c9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To drive away, disperse, shake off; said especially of tumors."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "338b6afd-ba10-4782-9750-389ff8bc17c5",
                                                "sense_id": "36e5d23a-e36f-3767-9e2d-05595b3173bf",
                                                "text": "For she was giuen all to fleshly lust, / And poured forth in sensuall delight, / That all regard of shame she had discust, / And meet respect of honour put to flight […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ea53d44a-e511-4223-a12e-6803d7847d05",
                                                "sense_id": "36e5d23a-e36f-3767-9e2d-05595b3173bf",
                                                "text": "If too much milke be the cauſe, then the Nurſe ſhall not give the childe ſucke ſo often, nor in ſuch plenty: If it proceed from wind, and that doe cauſe the childe to be thus troubled, it ſhall be diſcuſſed with Fomentations applied to the belly and navell; and with Carminative Cliſters, which ſhall bee given him, […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a573199e-10de-440f-900f-4fd49b08271a",
                                                "sense_id": "36e5d23a-e36f-3767-9e2d-05595b3173bf",
                                                "text": "June 15, 1751, Samuel Johnson, letter in The Rambler\nThe softness of my hands was secured by medicated gloves, and my bosom rubbed with a pomade prepared by my mother, of virtue to discuss pimples, and clear discolourations."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d6341e45-71f5-49ee-827e-ca1349ccbb32",
                                                "sense_id": "36e5d23a-e36f-3767-9e2d-05595b3173bf",
                                                "text": "Many arts were used to discuss the beginnings of new affliction."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
